"So my dear friend is happily married. Everything went relatively smoothly with a few bumps but she's done it. We had a wonderful time getting all dressed up, taking tons of pictures, meeting relatives, and enjoying a night of dinner and dancing. As I was participating in all of the prewedding and wedding events I began to really wonder what purpose the wedding serves and then what purpose marriage has. I'll start with weddings. I guess that weddings give people a chance to publicly proclaim their love and commitment to eachother. Also weddings are a time where two families come together and welcome the bride or groom into the family. And it's a good excuse for a party where long lost relatives can come and be reunited. These things are all wonderful, but it sort of takes the focus away from the bride and groom and just on to having a good time. Then there is always the possibility of eloping. I think this is much more romantic, however it comes at the cost of not including friends and family. But you could always include them later in a big party or something. I'm still debating this one. Personally I'd like to have a wedding celebration some day but at the same time I know the stress and anxiety involved and I have to wonder if it's worth it. Okay so on to marriage. Hmm.. I'm not even sure where to begin. I guess I'll just say that I've always been curious to know what exactly they council people about in premarital councilling. Do they go through all the vows and make sure that both partners actually understand them all and will do all the things they say they will. Do they ask you if you think it's true love. What if you want to change your mind after that? My own personal thought on marriage is that it's a commitment to another person that you will be with them forever no matter what (unless someone breaks their vows) and you absolutely have to be in love - real true love. Then the question becomes how do you know if you are in love. Well I've decided to mentally make a list of all the things I think love is. I started out with the basics: Love is patient, love is kind, love is not selfish or keeps no record of wrongs etc. And then added a few things that I personally think love is. For example: love is being able to take care of a your partner when they are sick and need help on the bed pan after falling down on some icy stairs and breaking a tailbone. I guess that fits into the sickness and health one... My point is that a checklist of criteria for true love may just be a good idea to help you realize if your relationship is really meant to be or not. And if you are ready to commit to being married. Sometimes I think that love is blind and people get married with their eyes wide shut. Not me...I want the truth...I want true love.",0,normal,blog_corpus,3367344.female.23.indUnk.Taurus,,,,,,0
