"Listen to this (audio-only) clip of a brief snippet from CBS' 60 Minutes . It's clear at the end that Mary J. Blige was going to say, ""shit,"" whether or not you hear the whole word. The guy who got Howard Stern kicked off Clear Channel stations has submitted an FCC complaint on the broadcast . Whether or not you like it, certain words have crept into our vernacular. Bono and Blige -- who have used the words ""fuck"" and ""shit"" during recent broadcasts (OK, Bono's was a little over a year ago) as exclamatory phrases -- are not some young trash-mouthed kids with 10-word vocabularies. They're intelligent adults who have influence not just on the way kids see the world today, but in Bono's case, the world's politicians listen. I think we're moving into an age when context does matter. Bono's declaration that his receipt of an award was ""fucking brilliant"" was obviously an off-the-cuff remark with absolutely, positively, no intent to offend anyone. That particular broadcast was live with no delay, so censors could not have cut him off. And they shouldn't have, anyway. It just happens that that's the way Bono speaks. So what? He wasn't describing sexual acts or excretory functions -- which is what the law says is obscene -- so just let it go. Blige's exclamation, on the other hand, is a different situation. CBS had her saying, ""shit,"" on tape, and could have edited it out. But you know what? It was an exclamation of disappointment, did not describe sexual acts or excretory functions, and furthermore, was broadcast on a show targeted at adults. Come on, adults, grow up. This is the way people talk now, and maybe broadcast regulations -- and some people's attitudes -- could be brought up to speed. ""Damn"" used to be unbearably offensive, and we've mostly grown past that now. Let's grow past ""shit"" and ""fuck"" as exclamations. Let's take words in context, and let's not go overboard. E-mail me .",0,normal,blog_corpus,3136900.male.27.Communications-Media.Scorpio,,,,,,0
