"Matt Yglesias points out that trashing Michael Moore increases the amount of money going to left-wing causes, and probably also increases the number of Moore imitators. We've heard a lot about the ""polarization"" of the country lately, but I feel that applies mainly to Hollywood of late. Voters are increasingly the same, they just hate the little things in someone else's candidate -- the candidates are polarized, not the voters. On the other hand, there are people who hate people like Moore, and trash him in print, which makes more people go to see his movies. I happen to be one of those people who sees Moore's films, and I tend to like the message, but not the method. The trailers I've seen for Fahrenheit 9/11 reaffirm that, and I plan to see the film tomorrow night, if I can corral some tickets. The same kind of ""polarization"" effect happened recently with Mel Gibson's film The Passion of the Christ . The film would have had a built-in audience that would have been big, but not huge. However, with people saying the film was too violent or anti-Semitic, people who would not have seen the film did. I didn't manage to see it thanks to a busy schedule, but darned if I wouldn't have been in the second group -- I had no real interest in the movie, but when Jewish leaders decried it as anti-Semitic, I felt that if I wanted to chime in on that point (and I did want to, since these people claim to represent me), I had to go see the movie.",0,normal,blog_corpus,3136900.male.27.Communications-Media.Scorpio,,,,,,0
