"This flash movie is an amusing, dire warning from my favorite chicken little protest group, the ACLU . There's a large difference between pooling public data and infringing on the public's liberty. This is somehow missed whenever a politician decides to end his career and propose national ID cards. How is a database that contains my name, address, phone number, thumb print, eye color, height, and weight anything other than a convenience? It's information already dispersed in the public realm. Crime Reports are available to the public and often published daily in local newspapers. Speaking in sweeping generalities, most definably hazardous jobs are rewarded with bonus hazard pay, a right that many unions are happy to fight for. Why is there protest if state governments want to network their crime records? It would tremendously powerful crime fighting tool, a wealth of information available to every cop in a squad car. No longer would a bank robber from Missouri be given only a warning when he's pulled over without a break-light in Kansas. A more slippery slope... The integration of medical records and insurance information will be a welcomed improvement for anyone who has ever filled out another damn clipboard or needed duplicate medical tests because originals could not be found. Underneath all the fluff, the ACLU does highlight a real possibility. There will unlikely be any ""High Cholesterol Meatball Sub Surcharge,"" but just as insurance companies offer discounts for customers who drive safer cares, if the technology existed, they'd offer discounts for customers who make it a habit to go to the gym and eat healthy. A government mandate that forces all customers to adhere to such a plan is certainly a violation of their rights, but an opt-in plan would be a blessing for those tired of paying the high healthcare costs of some schlub whose three square meals a day are Wendy's Hamburgers. The bottom line is that these technologies will exist, and in my opinion, they should exist. Each improvement allows corportations to provide better service and tailored prices. This is capitalism. It's neither than problem nor the concern... ""While the private company that runs the [Multistate Anti-Terrorism Information Exchange] program has been very secretive about what data MATRIX contains, government sources report that it includes names and address of family members, property ownership, marine vessels, bankruptcies, liens and judgments, voter registrations, and criminal offender information."" This, I believe is all public data. The real debate is of BIG Government vs. small gov't. Who should hold this mass of data? While it may seem ominous for the Government to hold all this information about its citizens, the Government exists to serve the public and a series of checks and balances are in order to give us at least some power over our data. Private companies exist to generate profits. There aren't even any shareholders. ""However, given the amount of information that is available in today’s commercial databases, even more details of your private life might be captured and catalogued."" It's a scare tactic, but it's true. But there's probably no one out there reading this who hasn't used a supermarket value card to get a discount off their groceries. The concern shouldn't be the technology, the concern is that sufficient guidelines, user permissions, and ""chinese walls"" in place. (A ""Chinese wall"" is an intra-corporation barrier restricting the passing of information between two seperate entities that would give each other unfair advantages in a public market. For instance, the credit department of Goldman Sachs can't tell the Equities traders of any propriety, non-public credit information that would influence Equities traders' decisions in the market.) This may be so difficult as to yield the technology valueless. I don't know. But I wish the ACLU would focus its lawyers on these questions instead of using the database's ""ominous name"" as a chance to gain ground on its perpetual fight against the government. Sometimes I'm left thinking the ACLU is just looking for a good fight, or a good target, rather than looking to fight for my liberties.",0,normal,blog_corpus,3198682.male.23.indUnk.Sagittarius,,,,,,0
